## Ubuntu: start automatically on boot

From the *generated project* as your ordinary login account:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install redis-server
sudo systemctl enable --now redis-server
uv sync
cp .env.example .env
uv run --env-file .env {{ cli_command }} init-db
sudo bash deploy/install.sh
```

This writes three system-wide units: `{{ project_slug }}-api.service`, `{{ project_slug }}-worker.service` and `{{ project_slug }}-scheduler.service`. All start at boot and restart automatically on failure. The installer uses `SUDO_USER` as the non-root service account; override using `sudo SERVICE_USER=youruser bash deploy/install.sh` if needed. It requires an existing local virtual environment and `.env`. It does not automatically download packages or alter your Python installation.

```bash
sudo systemctl restart {{ project_slug }}-api.service
sudo systemctl status {{ project_slug }}-worker.service
sudo journalctl -fu {{ project_slug }}-scheduler.service
sudo systemctl disable --now {{ project_slug }}-{api,worker,scheduler}.service
```

Run `sudo systemctl daemon-reload` after manually changing unit files. To upgrade application code, run `uv sync --frozen --no-dev` as the service user and restart the units. Commit `uv.lock` to each generated service repository after the first `uv sync`.

The built-in `schedule` component is deliberately lightweight and in-memory. Missed executions during downtime are not replayed. ARQ jobs already enqueued to Redis can be processed following a restart, subject to Redis persistence and job expiry configuration. For exact/durable schedules, choose a persistent scheduler rather than assuming `schedule` guarantees it.

## Tests and CI

```bash
uv run ruff check .
uv run ruff format --check .
uv run pytest -q
```

The generated `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs on pull requests only and delegates to `RuchirChawdhry/ci-workflows`. The Copier template repository itself has no active workflows. See `AGENTS.md` for test-first agent guidance.
